senses.gloss:
sūryanamaskāra
(‘salutations to the sun’)
------------------------------------------
The Hindus always believed in a holistic concept of health. They considered the body and the मिन्द् as one unit, each acting and reacting on the other.
The सूर्यनमस्कार is one of the earliest techniques discovered and developed by them which accorded with their concept of health.
It has twelve postures, each representing a yogāsana (See YOGĀSANAS.) and a kind of salutation to the sun.
The Hindu scriptures call the sun आदित्य and describe twelve aspects of the same. They are actually different names of the same sun associated with the twelve signs of the Zodiac. Each has a name such as मित्र, Ravi, and so on. These names are converted into mantras (holy sentences) and used for each step.
The सूर्यनमस्कार has to be practised in the early morning, facing the rising sun, to the accompaniment of the respective mantras. The actual method and technique has to be learnt from an adept in the field.
The twelve postures along with the योगासनस् they represent as also the associated mantras are as follows:
Praṇāmāsana (prayer pose);
om mitrāya namaḥ.
Hastauttānāsana (raised-arms pose);
oṁ ravaye namaḥ.
Pādahastāsana (hand-to-foot pose);
oṁ sūryāya namaḥ.
Aśvasañcalanāsana (equestrian pose);
om bhānave namaḥ.
Śvānāsana (dog pose);
oṁ khagāya namaḥ.
Aṣṭāṅganamaskārāsana (obeisance with eight limbs);
om pūṣṇe namaḥ.
Bhujaṅgāsana (serpent-pose);
oṁ hiraṇyagarbhāya namaḥ.
Śvānāsana (dog-pose);
om marīcaye namaḥ.
Aśvasañcalanāsana (equestrian pose);
om ādityāya namaḥ.
Pādahastāsana (hand-to-foot pose);
oṁ savitre namaḥ.
Hasta-uttānāsana (raised arms pose);
om arkāya namaḥ.
Praṇāmāsana (prayer pose);
om bhāskarāya namaḥ.
